    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Canaanites honoured a pantheon headed by El and his consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal Hadad</a>, the storm god who brought the rain, and Anat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resheph">Resheph</a> among the other deities.</li><li>Most of what is known comes from the clay tablets of Ugarit, dated around 1275 BCE, which preserve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Cycle and other myths with hymns, prayers and ritual texts.</li><li>In the first millennium BCE its branches lived on among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, Arameans and Moabites, many scholars count Israelite Yahwism among them, and Phoenician settlers carried it west as the Punic religion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Cycle the storm god Baal is swallowed by Mot, Death, and a terrible drought follows, until the goddess Anat attacks Mot with a knife, grinds him to pieces and scatters him, so that Baal can return and refresh the earth with rain.</p></aside>
    </section>

