    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">3rd millennium BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> tablets from Tell Mardikh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> list offerings to El, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>, the earliest evidence of Canaanite religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">15th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>, shed further light on Canaanite religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1275 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Ugaritic texts, more than three hundred of them on Canaanite myths, rites and prayers, date from about this time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Ugarit is destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and other Punic communities in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 850 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a> is set up in Moab, one of the few sources for religion in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> beyond Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd–2nd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> conquers the Punic lands, where Punic religious practice continues</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">4th century CE</span><span class="rf-text">The last Punic religious practices die out</span></li></ol>
    </section>

