    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ebla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ebla.jpg" alt="Ebla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HPIM3078_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mappo, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Canaanite religion was the religion of the peoples of the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> during approximately the first three millennia BCE. Knowledge of it is fragmentary, too thin for a complete, continuous account. Until Levantine archaeology began in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, it was known mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and from later Greek writers: Lucian’s On the Syrian Goddess, fragments of the Phoenician History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a>, and Damascius.</p><p>The earliest evidence comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> tablets, clay texts found at Tell Mardikh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and dated to the second half of the 3rd millennium BCE, which list offerings to the chief Canaanite gods El, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>. In the late 3rd and early 2nd millennia BCE the same gods appear in the personal names of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a>, whose homeland lay in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> regions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> layers at Tell es-Safi hold donkeys, sheep and goats imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to be sacrificed; a complete donkey beneath a building’s foundations may have been a foundation deposit.</p><p>The religion grew among more powerful and populous neighbours and shows the clear influence of Mesopotamian and Egyptian practice. Robert G. Boling argued that Canaan had no “local pantheon”: on a land bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, its people chose the most important and interesting gods of their neighbours, gave them several names and dropped their places of origin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/asherah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asherah.jpg" alt="Asherah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hecht_Museum,_Israel_%E2%80%93_figurines_010.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hanay, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Canaanites honoured gods in a four-tier hierarchy headed by El, also called Elyon, “Most High”, god of creation, and his consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a> (Athirat). Among the others were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal Hadad</a>, lord of storms, thunder and lightning and king of the gods; Anat, virgin goddess of war; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, goddess of war, hunting and love; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>, god of grain and father of Baal; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resheph">Resheph</a>, god of plague and of healing; Yam, god of the sea and rivers; the sun goddess Shapash; the moon god Yarikh; the craftsman Kothar-wa-Khasis; and Mot, Death, who was given no worship or offerings. Families focused on veneration of the dead through household gods and goddesses, the Elohim.</p><p>No cosmology survives from Ugarit. In the later account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a>, said to come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchuniathon">Sanchuniathon</a> of Berytus, the creator Elion was father of the gods and married Beruth, “the city”, as other gods were linked with theirs: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemosh">Chemosh</a> with Moab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Pairs of mountains recur in the myths.</p><p>Images of gods such as Baal stood in shrines, often on hilltops or “high places” among groves of trees, with standing stones or pillars, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> condemns. Animals were sacrificed. After death the npš, usually translated “soul”, departed to the land of Mot. The dead were buried with grave goods and given food and drink so that they would not trouble the living, and dead relatives were venerated and sometimes asked for help.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesha-stele.jpg" alt="Mesha Stele" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1120870_Louvre_st%C3%A8le_de_M%C3%A9sha_AO5066_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By far the richest source is the Ugaritic texts, clay tablets found since 1928 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Shamra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, ancient Ugarit, a city destroyed around 1200 BCE; they are dated around 1275 BCE. More than three hundred of them preserve key myths and legends, with incantations, prayers, hymns, votive texts, lists of gods and sacrifices, festival catalogues, ritual prescriptions, liturgies and omen texts. Further light comes from the inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a>, the 15th-century BCE king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>, and the tablets of Emar of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE.</p><p>The best-known myth is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Cycle. Baal Hadad defeats the sea god Yam with two magic weapons, “Driver” and “Chaser”, made by Kothar-wa-Khasis, and with the help of Athirat and Anat persuades El to let him have a palace. From its window he challenges Mot, who swallows him and takes him to the underworld; drought follows, until Anat grinds Mot to pieces and Baal returns with the rain.</p><p>The first millennium BCE left inscriptions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a> of Moab (c. 850 BCE) and the Deir Alla inscription (c. 700 BCE); Aramean texts such as the Tell Fekherya bilingual, the Stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakkur">Zakkur</a> (c. 775 BCE) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sefire-steles">Sefire steles</a>; Phoenician ones such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yehimilk">Yehimilk</a> inscription and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karatepe">Karatepe</a> bilingual (c. 720 BCE); and the Punic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Marseille Tariffs (c. 200 BCE). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a> (c. 64–141 CE), known through Eusebius, wrote after much Greek and Roman influence, so it is hard to tell what shaped his account.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshmun.jpg" alt="Eshmun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eshmun_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BlingBling10 at en.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> the religion took in the gods of its neighbours. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> period, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-mounted maryannu ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> was identified with the Egyptian god Set and was thereafter shown wearing the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> and Anat were given Hathor-like wigs. In the Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> Hurrian and Mitannian influence was strong: the Hurrian goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebat">Hebat</a> was worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and Baal was held equivalent to the Hurrian storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a> and the Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarhunt">Tarhunt</a>. Baal Hadad and El can be distinguished among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a>, who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, as Egyptian power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> declined, peoples and cities formed regional and national cults round a patron god: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemosh">Chemosh</a> of the Moabites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milcom">Milcom</a> of the Ammonites, Qos of the Edomites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>. Several branches persisted through the first millennium BCE, among them Aramean and Phoenician religion.</p><p>From the 9th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and other Punic communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, where Punic religion continued the Phoenician branch with local differences. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Greek religion spread alongside Canaanite traditions in the non-Jewish parts of Canaan, and Canaanite gods took the names of their Greek equivalents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered the Punic lands in the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, but Punic practices continued, in some places until the 4th century CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>“At the center of Canaanite religion,” in one scholar’s summary, “was royal concern for religious and political legitimacy and the imposition of a divinely ordained legal structure, as well as peasant emphasis on fertility of the crops, flocks, and humans.” Kings played an important religious role and, in ceremonies such as the sacred marriage of the New Year, may have been revered as gods. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, cults of patron gods bound together the new political and communal identities of cities and peoples.</p><p>Family duty carried religious weight. Children were expected to care for their parents, and sons were responsible for burying them and keeping up their tombs. Funerary rites honoured the dead and fed the npš on its way to the land of Mot, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, libations, music and devotional songs, and sometimes trance rituals, oracles and necromancy. Graves at Tel Megiddo held many wine vessels and jars of beeswax, animal fat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, resin and even vanilla, perhaps for a funerary feast or as offerings, and evidence of opium use comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> site in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><p>A clear picture of practice is almost impossible to reconstruct, but surrounding peoples knew of Canaanite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a>. K. L. Noll writes that in military crisis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> was offered to the patron god of a besieged city, and prisoners of war to the victorious god. Ronald Hendel holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> disparaged Canaanite religion because they wished to set themselves apart from their Canaanite ancestors and form a new national identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Canaanite religion ended with its last branch, the Punic religion of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, which outlived the Roman conquest and survived in some places until the 4th century CE. In the east its gods had already blended with Greek ones in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, taking Greek names.</p><p>Its influence ran in several directions. Carried west by Phoenician sailors, Canaanite ideas can be seen in Greek mythology: the division of the world between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> and Hades mirrors that between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, Yam and Mot, and the Labours of Hercules mirror the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a>, who was often equated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. Words and places kept its traces too: the npš of the funerary rites is the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> word נפש, Bethoron in ancient Israel takes its name from the underworld god Horon, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> was likely the cult centre of the moon god Yarikh.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> distinguishes the Yahwism of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> from Canaanite religion, but many scholars count it among the Canaanite religions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> as a Canaanite people. Scholars still debate details, such as the claim that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a> was considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>’s consort: Edward Lipiński argues that the asherah of the inscriptions was a holy place rather than a goddess. Most of what is known today comes from archaeology, above all the tablets of Ugarit.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Canaanite religion was the polytheistic religion of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> during about three millennia BCE, its pantheon headed by El and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, Anat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>. Known chiefly from the tablets of Ugarit, it honoured the dead, worshipped on high places and took in its neighbours’ gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> carried it west, where its Punic branch lasted until the 4th century CE.</em></p>

